{"kpis":[{"id":"kpi_01","name":"Overall Churn Rate","cat":"Retention","value":0.9404,"display":"94.0%","unit":"%","defn":"Share of members whose latest billing cycle ended more than 30 days before the pipeline as-of date (2026-08-14).","note":"Read with kpi_15. The pipeline date sits 313 days past the last real event, so this figure is inflated by missing data, not only by behaviour.","pair":"kpi_15"},{"id":"kpi_02","name":"Overall Retention Rate","cat":"Retention","value":0.0596,"display":"6.0%","unit":"%","defn":"1 minus the overall churn rate.","note":"The mirror of kpi_01, so it carries the same as-of caveat.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_03","name":"Active Members","cat":"Retention","value":1011,"display":"1,011","unit":"members","defn":"Members not classified as churned as of the pipeline as-of date.","note":"This is the population the composite risk score runs on: Low 487, Moderate 403, Elevated 109, High 12.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_04","name":"7-Day Activation Rate","cat":"Acquisition & Activation","value":0.1875,"display":"18.8%","unit":"%","defn":"Share of members who log any gym or app event within 7 days of their join date.","note":"The largest single drop in the funnel: 81.2% of members never engage in week one.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_05","name":"Renewal Follow-Through Rate","cat":"Acquisition & Activation","value":0.4226,"display":"42.3%","unit":"%","defn":"Of members who ever subscribed, the share who renewed at least once more.","note":"Healthy next to activation. Renewal is not where the platform leaks.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_06","name":"Median Revenue per Member","cat":"Revenue","value":8970,"display":"₹8,970","unit":"INR","defn":"Median total rupees paid per member. Median is preferred because spend is heavily right-skewed.","note":"Quote this one when you need a typical member.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_07","name":"Mean Revenue per Member","cat":"Revenue","value":18711.32,"display":"₹18,711","unit":"INR","defn":"Mean total rupees paid per member, reported next to the median to show the skew.","note":"Roughly double the median: a few large payers pull the mean up.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_08","name":"Discount Utilisation Rate","cat":"Revenue","value":0.3033,"display":"30.3%","unit":"%","defn":"Share of distinct renewal records with a discount applied.","note":"","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_09","name":"Corporate Tie-Up Mix","cat":"Revenue","value":0.2058,"display":"20.6%","unit":"%","defn":"Share of distinct renewal records under a corporate tie-up plan.","note":"","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_10","name":"Median Plan-to-Engagement Ratio","cat":"Revenue","value":2520,"display":"₹2,520","unit":"INR / session","defn":"Median rupees paid per logged gym session. Surfaces members who are paying but not showing up.","note":"The twelve flagged members sit at 48,000 to 102,000 on this measure.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_11","name":"Avg Weekly Session Rate","cat":"Engagement","value":0.3,"display":"0.30","unit":"sessions / week","defn":"Mean of each member's rolling 4-week sessions per week.","note":"","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_12","name":"Avg Session Consistency Score","cat":"Engagement","value":0.77,"display":"0.77","unit":"CV","defn":"Mean coefficient of variation of the gaps between sessions. Lower means more regular attendance.","note":"The name is misleading: a lower score is better.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_13","name":"Avg Streak Length","cat":"Product / Streak Health","value":36.0,"display":"36.0","unit":"days","defn":"Mean of each member's average streak length.","note":"","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_14","name":"Streak-Break Recovery Rate","cat":"Product / Streak Health","value":0.148,"display":"14.8%","unit":"%","defn":"Share of finished streaks followed by a gym check-in within 14 days (streak grain).","note":"Over all finished streaks in the raw table the figure is 10.3%. The two differ because of the denominator.","pair":null},{"id":"kpi_15","name":"Churn Rate (data-realistic as-of)","cat":"Retention","value":0.8401,"display":"84.0%","unit":"%","defn":"The same churn logic anchored at the last real event in the data, 2025-10-05.","note":"Treat this as the operationally meaningful number and always show it beside kpi_01.","pair":"kpi_01"}],"insights":[{"id":"INS-01","title":"Churn looks catastrophic at 94%, but much of it is a measurement artefact","aud":"Leadership / Finance","conf":"High","confN":4,"story":"The pipeline's fixed as-of date (2026-08-14) sits well past the dataset's actual last event (2025-10-05), so almost every member reads as churned. Re-anchoring to a data-realistic date drops churn to 84.0%. That is still high, but the 94.0% headline should never be quoted to leadership without this context.","evidence":"kpi_01 against kpi_15 in kpi_summary.csv; the PR9 and PR11 as-of decision.","impact":"High. Wrong framing risks over-reacting, for example with panic win-back spend, to a partly artefactual number.","action":"Report churn with both figures side by side. Treat kpi_15 as the operationally meaningful number.","route":"overview","anchor":"two-clocks"},{"id":"INS-02","title":"Activation, not renewal, is where the platform loses members","aud":"Product / Growth","conf":"Medium-High","confN":3,"story":"Only 18.8% of members log any activity within 7 days of joining. That is an 81.2% drop at a single stage and the largest of any funnel step. No acquisition channel or demographic segment explains it: every Cramér's V is below 0.1.","evidence":"kpi_04; activation_segment_comparison.csv; the PR10 funnel.","impact":"High. This is the single highest-leverage retention lever found in the dataset.","action":"Prioritise onboarding experiments on UX and timing over channel-targeted acquisition spend.","route":"activation","anchor":"funnel"},{"id":"INS-03","title":"Activation is quietly improving, so the funnel is not getting worse","aud":"Leadership / Product","conf":"Medium","confN":2,"story":"Monthly cohort activation rose from about 14.6% in late 2024 to about 36.6% in September 2025. It is the most encouraging non-obvious finding in the dataset and the opposite of what the 81.2% headline drop-off implies in isolation.","evidence":"activation_cohort_trend.csv; PR12 section 12.1(b).","impact":"Medium. It reframes the story from a broken funnel to an improving one worth investing in.","action":"Investigate what changed operationally in early 2025 and double down on it.","route":"activation","anchor":"cohort"},{"id":"INS-04","title":"Churn and activation are platform-wide problems, not a branch-operations issue","aud":"Operations","conf":"Medium","confN":2,"story":"None of the 40 branches is a statistical churn-rate outlier, and none combines a churn anomaly with bottom-quartile activation. That rules out a few bad branches as the root cause.","evidence":"branch_risk_summary.csv (0 of 40 flagged); PR11 section 11.2.2 and PR12 section 12.1(c).","impact":"Medium. It redirects the investigation away from branch staffing and operations, toward product and onboarding.","action":"Do not fund a branch-level operational audit as a churn fix. Focus centrally on onboarding.","route":"risk","anchor":"branches"},{"id":"INS-05","title":"A small, well-defined 'paid once, never engaged' cohort is ready for outreach","aud":"Customer Success","conf":"High","confN":4,"story":"Twelve currently-active members trigger nearly every composite risk signal. Most are single-event members paying 48,000 to 102,000 rupees per logged session. It is a short, actionable list rather than a diffuse population-wide signal.","evidence":"at_risk_members.csv (12 rows); PR11 section 11.2.3.","impact":"Medium. The volume is small, but the win-back value per member is high and the targeting cost is low.","action":"Reach out directly, by call or PT trial offer, to the 12 flagged members within the next billing cycle.","route":"risk","anchor":"at-risk"}],"risk":[{"id":"MBR-000763","branch":"BR-039","recency":395,"events":9,"slope":-0.0595,"ratio":48206.6667,"sig":[1,0,1,1],"sample":fa